From: Balázs Gyimesi <gyimesi.balazs@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: mhi_net: page backed RX buffers

A 32 KiB MRU makes netdev_alloc_skb() fall back to kmalloc (the size is
above SKB_WITH_OVERHEAD(PAGE_SIZE), 3776 bytes on arm64), and above
KMALLOC_MAX_CACHE_SIZE (8 KiB with 4 KiB pages) kmalloc() is itself a
page allocator call: netdev_alloc_skb(32768) asks for SKB_HEAD_ALIGN(32768
+ NET_SKB_PAD) = 33152 bytes, an order-4 (64 KiB) block, and asks for it
with GFP_ATOMIC although the refill runs in a workqueue. Allocate the
block directly and build the skb around it with build_skb() instead: with
GFP_KERNEL, so an order-3 refill may reclaim and compact instead of
failing, and sized for 128 bytes of headroom (ARCH_DMA_MINALIGN, so
skb->data is on a DMA cache-line boundary), the MRU rounded up to 128,
and skb_shared_info (320 bytes on arm64 with 17 frags, SKB_DATA_ALIGN'd).
The block is handed back through the page-frag path (head_frag,
skb_free_frag) when the skb is freed. For the MRU this driver runs with
here (32256, the pci_generic table entry) that is 128 + 32256 + 320 =
32704 bytes, an order-3 (32 KiB) block; an MRU of 32768 would need an
order-4 (64 KiB) block with half of it unused, and order 4 is above
PAGE_ALLOC_COSTLY_ORDER, so the refill would also be the kind of
allocation the page allocator gives up on sooner. The 16 KiB MRU this
patch was first written for was an order-3 block as well (16832 bytes),
half of it unused.

For the 32256-byte MRU the device writes offsets 128..32384 of the 32 KiB
block and build_skb() puts skb_shared_info at 32448 (the block's end minus
320), so the DMA and the metadata the CPU writes never share a cache
line (the same holds for any MRU: the block is sized with the whole
skb_shared_info after the DMA-aligned payload).

Each buffer gets a block of its own rather than a slice of the shared
page-frag cache, since it is a DMA target. The consumer on the QMAP link
(rmnet) copies the packets out of the frame anyway, so whether the frame
is page backed makes no difference to it.
---
--- a/drivers/net/mhi_net.c
+++ b/drivers/net/mhi_net.c
@@ -265,6 +265,65 @@
 		netif_wake_queue(ndev);
 }
 
+/* headroom of an RX buffer, a whole DMA cache line */
+#define MHI_NET_RX_PAD		ARCH_DMA_MINALIGN
+
+/* A page block of its own for every RX buffer, built into an skb with
+ * build_skb(). netdev_alloc_skb() uses kmalloc above a page, and kmalloc
+ * above 8 KiB is a page-allocator call: for a 32 KiB MRU
+ * SKB_HEAD_ALIGN(32768 + NET_SKB_PAD) = 33152 bytes is an order-4 (64 KiB)
+ * block, requested GFP_ATOMIC from a workqueue. Here the block is asked
+ * for with GFP_KERNEL and holds 128 bytes of headroom (ARCH_DMA_MINALIGN:
+ * skb->data on a DMA cache-line boundary), the MRU rounded up to 128, and
+ * skb_shared_info (320 bytes on arm64, SKB_DATA_ALIGN'd): e.g. 32704 bytes
+ * for a 32256-byte MRU, an order-3 (32 KiB) block, in which the device's
+ * writes (offsets 128..32384) and skb_shared_info (at 32448) never share
+ * a cache line. Freed through the page-frag path
+ * (head_frag). Not a slice of the shared page-frag cache: the buffer is a
+ * DMA target, and the cache would hand the rest of its page to
+ * allocations the CPU is writing while the device fills ours.
+ */
+static struct sk_buff *mhi_net_alloc_rx_skb(struct net_device *ndev,
+					    unsigned int size)
+{
+	unsigned int truesize, order;
+	struct sk_buff *skb;
+	struct page *page;
+	void *data;
+
+	truesize = ALIGN(MHI_NET_RX_PAD + size, ARCH_DMA_MINALIGN) +
+		   SKB_DATA_ALIGN(sizeof(struct skb_shared_info));
+	/* 3 for an MRU up to 32256; 32768 (the usual pci_generic default)
+	 * already needs order 4, a 64 KiB block with half of it unused, and
+	 * a costly-order allocation. Not an error, vanilla would allocate
+	 * the same block, so just note it.
+	 */
+	order = get_order(truesize);
+	if (unlikely(order > PAGE_ALLOC_COSTLY_ORDER))
+		netdev_info_once(ndev, "RX buffers are order-%u (%lu-byte) blocks for an MRU of %u, above the %lu-byte costly-order limit\n",
+				 order, PAGE_SIZE << order, size,
+				 PAGE_SIZE << PAGE_ALLOC_COSTLY_ORDER);
+	/* Not worth reclaim retries or the OOM killer, which an order-3 request
+	 * would otherwise risk (it is not a costly order): the caller stops
+	 * refilling and tries again shortly.
+	 */
+	page = alloc_pages(GFP_KERNEL | __GFP_COMP | __GFP_NOWARN | __GFP_NORETRY,
+			   order);
+	if (unlikely(!page))
+		return NULL;
+
+	data = page_address(page);
+	skb = build_skb(data, PAGE_SIZE << order);
+	if (unlikely(!skb)) {
+		__free_pages(page, order);
+		return NULL;
+	}
+
+	skb_reserve(skb, MHI_NET_RX_PAD);
+	skb->dev = ndev;
+	return skb;
+}
+
 static void mhi_net_rx_refill_work(struct work_struct *work)
 {
 	struct mhi_net_dev *mhi_netdev = container_of(work, struct mhi_net_dev,
@@ -278,7 +337,7 @@
 	size = mhi_netdev->mru ? mhi_netdev->mru : READ_ONCE(ndev->mtu);
 
 	while (!mhi_queue_is_full(mdev, DMA_FROM_DEVICE)) {
-		skb = netdev_alloc_skb(ndev, size);
+		skb = mhi_net_alloc_rx_skb(ndev, size);
 		if (unlikely(!skb))
 			break;
 
